//! lotus/move.rye -- ALES113: move -- lift the `count` samples starting at `start` out of the timeline and reinsert them at `dest`, IN PLACE, so the span is RELOCATED and the
//! clip's length HOLDS. This is the relocate-twin of ALES112's duplicate: where duplicate COPIES a span (a second copy lands beside the first and the clip GROWS), move CARRIES
//! one span somewhere new (the span leaves its old spot and the clip stays the same length). Circle a bar, drag it, and it lifts out of one place and lands at another -- the verse
//! pulled ahead of the chorus, the stray breath slid to a rest, the loop nudged whole to a new beat. Naming it beside duplicate completes the copy/relocate pair on a selection:
//! repeat it where it is, or carry it somewhere new.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: move(clip, start, count, dest) copies the span [start, start+count) into one bounded scratch buffer, then ALES2's cut removes the span in place (the
//! clip shrinks by count), then ALES2's splice reinserts the saved span at `dest` (the clip grows back by count) -- so the net length is unchanged and no third primitive is
//! invented. `dest` names a position in the timeline AFTER the span is removed (0 ≤ dest ≤ len − count); because the head [0, start) has length `start`, dest = start is the
//! identity and the inverse is exactly move(dest, count, start). Every range is refused BadRange BEFORE any write, reusing ALES2's EditError whole. One bounded scratch buffer.
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE MOVE LAW: after move(clip, start, count, dest) the clip is the removed timeline (original[0..start] ++ original[start+count..len)) with the span
//! reinserted at dest, checked forward, backward, and to the head. THE INVERSE LAW: move(start, count, dest) then move(dest, count, start) returns the original byte for byte --
//! move is its own inverse under swapped endpoints. THE PRESERVATION / PEAK LAW: move relocates existing samples and invents none, the length unchanged and the multiset of
//! samples unchanged, so the peak re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged. THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW: count = 0 the identity, dest = start the identity, a start past the
//! samples or a count past the remainder or a dest past len − count each BadRange, before any write.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES113.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-012632_fill-ales113-lotus-move.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, the EditError reused whole, and the cut + splice this rung composes; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) -- the
//! peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to prove that relocating existing samples never moves the peak.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It copies a span into one bounded scratch buffer and
//! reinserts it through the proven cut/splice -- it invents no sample, changes no value, and reads no byte past len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real
//! device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/move.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const move_version = "20260815.012632";

/// move forwards ALES2's OWN faults reused by name -- BadRange when the span falls outside the current samples or the destination falls outside the removed timeline -- and invents
/// none of its own; `start`, `count`, and `dest` are plain span/position bounds with no illegal value save the ranges the samples already name.
pub const MoveError = timeline.EditError;

/// Lift the `count` samples starting at `start` out of the timeline and reinsert them at `dest` (a position in the timeline after the span is removed, 0 ≤ dest ≤ len − count), in
/// place -- the span is relocated and the clip's length holds. Refuses BadRange when the span falls outside the current samples or `dest` falls outside the removed timeline, before
/// any write. This is ALES112's duplicate read as a relocation rather than a copy -- the copy/relocate twin on a selection.
pub fn move(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, dest: u32) MoveError!void {
    // invariant: the length never claims more samples than the buffer holds -- every seat we read is inside the buffer.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);

    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples (checked without underflow -- count against the remainder), refused by name before any write.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: the destination lies within the timeline that remains once the span is removed -- dest ≤ len − count, checked without underflow before any write.
    if (dest > clip.len - count) return error.BadRange;

    // Copy the span into one bounded scratch buffer before removing it -- its budget is max_clip, the same bound the Clip's own buffer names, so count ≤ len ≤ max_clip always fits.
    var scratch: [timeline.max_clip]i16 = [_]i16{0} ** timeline.max_clip;
    // invariant: the span we save never exceeds the scratch budget -- count is bounded by the current samples, themselves bounded by max_clip.
    assert(count <= scratch.len);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) scratch[i] = clip.buf[start + i];

    // Remove the span in place through ALES2's cut (the clip shrinks by count), then reinsert the saved span at `dest` through ALES2's splice (the clip grows back by count). The
    // net length is unchanged, so splice can never refuse ClipFull here; both are reached only after every range above passed, so no write precedes a refusal.
    try timeline.cut(clip, start, count);
    try timeline.splice(clip, dest, scratch[0..count]);

    // invariant: move relocated the span without changing the count of samples -- the length is back within its bound, exactly as it began.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE MOVE LAW -- the span is relocated, the length unchanged, proven forward, backward, and to the head; THE INVERSE LAW -- move then move(dest, count, start)
//     returns the original byte for byte; THE PRESERVATION / PEAK LAW -- move relocates existing samples, so the peak re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged; THE DEGENERATE /
//     REFUSAL LAW -- count 0 the identity, dest = start the identity, a span past the samples or a dest past len − count BadRange, before any write ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

/// The clip's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 -- the reader the peak law checks the result against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE MOVE LAW -- the span is relocated and the length is unchanged. Proven at a forward move, a backward move, and a move to the head.
    {
        // Forward: lift {20, 30} (start 1, count 2), reinsert at dest 2 of the removed timeline {10, 40, 50} -> {10, 40, 20, 30, 50}.
        var fwd = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&fwd, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try move(&fwd, 1, 2, 2);
        if (fwd.len != 5) return error.MoveFwdLength;
        if (!eq(fwd.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 40, 20, 30, 50 })) return error.MoveFwdWrong;

        // Backward: lift {40, 50} (start 3, count 2), reinsert at dest 1 of the removed timeline {10, 20, 30} -> {10, 40, 50, 20, 30}.
        var back = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&back, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try move(&back, 3, 2, 1);
        if (!eq(back.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 40, 50, 20, 30 })) return error.MoveBackWrong;

        // To the head: lift {30, 40} (start 2, count 2), reinsert at dest 0 of the removed timeline {10, 20, 50} -> {30, 40, 10, 20, 50}.
        var head = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&head, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try move(&head, 2, 2, 0);
        if (!eq(head.samples(), &[_]i16{ 30, 40, 10, 20, 50 })) return error.MoveHeadWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE INVERSE LAW -- move(start, count, dest) then move(dest, count, start) returns the original byte for byte, the span carried out and carried home.
    {
        const src = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &src);
        try move(&clip, 1, 2, 3); // lift {20, 30}, reinsert at 3 of {10, 40, 50} → {10, 40, 50, 20, 30}
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 40, 50, 20, 30 })) return error.MoveInverseSetup;
        try move(&clip, 3, 2, 1); // carry it home: lift {20, 30} from index 3, reinsert at 1
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &src)) return error.InverseBroken;
    }

    // 3. THE PRESERVATION / PEAK LAW -- move relocates existing samples and invents none, so the peak re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged: the loudest sample simply sits at a
    //    new index, the length unchanged.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 100, -30000, 200, -50, 75 });
        const before = try clip_peak(&clip);
        const len_before = clip.len;
        try move(&clip, 1, 1, 3); // carry the loud -30000 to a new index
        if (clip.len != len_before) return error.PeakLawLengthMoved;
        if (try clip_peak(&clip) != before) return error.PeakMoved;
    }

    // 4a. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count 0 is the identity, and dest = start is the identity (the span reinserted exactly where it was removed).
    {
        var zeroc = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&zeroc, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 });
        try move(&zeroc, 1, 0, 0); // zero count — the identity
        if (!eq(zeroc.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountMoved;

        var same = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&same, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9, 10 });
        try move(&same, 1, 2, 1); // dest = start — reinserted exactly where removed, the identity
        if (!eq(same.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9, 10 })) return error.SameDestMoved;
    }

    // 4b. THE REFUSAL LAW -- a `start` past the current samples, a `count` past the remainder, and a `dest` past len − count each refuse BadRange, before any write, the clip
    //     untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 });
        if (move(&clip, 5, 1, 0)) |_| return error.StartNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (move(&clip, 2, 5, 0)) |_| return error.CountNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        // dest past len − count: len 4, count 2, so dest must be ≤ 2; dest 3 refuses.
        if (move(&clip, 0, 2, 3)) |_| return error.DestNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        // The clip is untouched by the refused range calls -- each refusal came before any write.
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 })) return error.RefusedCallWrote;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-move: MOVE — lift the `count` samples starting at `start` out of the timeline and reinsert them at `dest`, IN PLACE, so the span is RELOCATED and the clip's " ++
        "length HOLDS; the relocate-twin of ALES112's duplicate (which COPIES a span, growing the clip): move CARRIES one span somewhere new. This is the drag gesture every " ++
        "editor ships — circle a bar, drag it, and it lifts out of one place and lands at another (the verse pulled ahead of the chorus, the loop nudged whole). The rule: copy " ++
        "the span into one bounded scratch buffer, then ALES2's cut removes it in place, then ALES2's splice reinserts it at dest; the net length is unchanged and no third " ++
        "primitive is invented. `dest` names a position in the removed timeline (0 ≤ dest ≤ len − count), so dest = start is the identity and the inverse is move(dest, count, " ++
        "start). Refuses BadRange for a start past the samples, a count past the remainder, or a dest past len − count, before any write. The crux is four laws. THE MOVE LAW: " ++
        "the clip is the removed timeline with the span reinserted at dest. THE INVERSE LAW: move then move(dest, count, start) returns the original byte for byte. THE " ++
        "PRESERVATION / PEAK LAW: move relocates existing samples and invents none, so the peak re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged. THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW: count 0 " ++
        "the identity, dest = start the identity, a bad range BadRange. Purely local — it copies a span and reinserts it through the proven cut/splice, inventing no sample, " ++
        "changing no value, reading no byte past len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
